**Read progressively:** [Metal Recycling: Opportunities, Limits, Infrastructure — UNEP International Resource Panel](../../../materials/metals-and-metallurgical-production/sources/Metal-Recycling-UNEP-IRP.lingua#r_40JXFY43ST442BB8MJEAYJT9N1) and [Tópicos sobre a Reciclagem de Metais e Sustentabilidade — Ronaldo Luiz Corrêa dos Santos](../../../materials/metals-and-metallurgical-production/sources/Reciclagem-de-Metais-e-Sustentabilidade-CETEM.lingua#r_7X28NJBWY8Z5Y1BVZXHVC4MHSX) in full; [The Materials Science behind Sustainable Metals and Alloys — Dierk Raabe](../sources/Materials-Science-behind-Sustainable-Metals-Raabe.lingua#r_6MXWN12M466MS374XY585GBHBN), the full review; [The U.S. Plastics Recycling Economy — Thomas, Kneifel and Butry](../sources/Plastics-Recycling-Economy-NIST.lingua#r_4FMXC9V02YNV4DFSCCV0CVR8JN), the full report. Continue through the existing metallurgical recovery Record.

A used product is a structured combination of materials, coatings, contaminants and information. Recovery changes that structure through disassembly, sorting, size reduction, separation and chemical or thermal processing. Material that enters a collection system does not all become an accepted input for the same function.

Trace several consequential elements or constituents through products and residuals. A mass balance can close while useful properties are lost. Conversely, a smaller recovered mass may preserve a demanding function when separation and processing are effective. Account for the work, energy, reagents, equipment and information needed to make the output usable.

**Work:** Draw one complete product-to-feed route and name each output's next function. Distinguish measured yields from assumed transfer fractions. Explain how a joining or formulation choice made during manufacture affects later separation. Keep environmental consequences as questions for the complete life-cycle account that follows.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
**Continue in materials engineering:** [Evaluate recycling through whole products, element pathways, and usable recovered material](../../../materials/metals-and-metallurgical-production/operation/Collection-sorting-recycling-metallurgy-and-quality-limits.lingua#r_5S3S2PXFEJMD6S0CEV434HSSNT).

